(* heap_no_store_guess_again.fl with a store: three lines more, which make a
   block that can change at the toplevel of the unit, pass it through
   [%opaque] and store into it. Both terms then have a primitive of the heap,
   and each of the four liftings to symbols of the validation lifts nothing in
   a term that it is given, where that term has one, whatever liftings the tool
   guesses. The guess made again, which validates the unit without the store,
   gives the same functions another guess: the unit is not validated, and the
   line under the verdict says that the two terms have a primitive of the heap.

   The second check, whose line begins "related once lifted", makes its
   liftings without the test of the heap. It comes to a guess made again too,
   and accepts with it. That line does not validate.

   What this does not show: that a guess made again can never lead the
   validation to accept a unit whose terms have a primitive of the heap. The
   validation may accept such a unit where no lifting is needed. And the test
   of a lifting is of the term that the lifting is given, not of the unit as
   it was read: a rewrite before the lifting may have removed the primitive.
   What a lifting does not do is lift in a term that has one when it is given
   that term. *)
